WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.640
Is it okay to punch a Nazi?

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:06.320
I mean we did go to war against them, so yeah.

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:09.980
This week's top story: If you see a Nazi,
punch a Nazi.

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:12.260
Liberals should not be raving about this.

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:14.519
A new segment we like to call fuck up a Nazi.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.540
My 90 year uncle, if he was alive would have
punched that son of a bitch in the face

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:18.760
and I woulda had his back.

00:00:18.769 --> 00:00:20.740
It was a hotly debated question for months

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:23.249
after video of white nationalist Richard Spencer

00:00:23.249 --> 00:00:26.909
getting punched in the middle of an interview
on the day of Donald Trump's inauguration

00:00:26.909 --> 00:00:30.100
went viral and inspired countless remixes.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.240
But the question was never really just about
Nazis

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:40.240
it was about the acceptability of political
violence.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:43.430
And in the following year, the question came
up again and again

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:47.100
as several high profile political protests turned violent.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.840
Including one on the campus of UC Berkeley.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.500
We visited Berkeley about a year after that
protest turned riot

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:58.540
to ask students whether punching Nazis, or
other deplorables is ever okay.

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:01.320
If they want to punch a Nazi, I'm not going
to judge.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:04.200
It depends on what you define as a Nazi.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:10.420
The people whose words create a culture that
sort of ratifies violence against a certain group

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:11.860
are punchable.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:17.660
On February 1st 2017 the nation watched the
University of California Berkeley burn

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:21.640
as masked members of the far left group Antifa
lit fires,

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:24.160
threw safety barriers through store windows

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.400
physically attacked people and threw rocks
at police.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:31.700
The protests turned riots were sparked by
the arrival of right wing provocateur

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.060
Milo Yiannopoulos

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:36.160
invited to campus by a conservative student
group.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:39.480
President Trump helped ignite the controversy
by tweeting a threat

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:41.460
to pull federal funding from Berkeley.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.960
Protests continued throughout the rest of
the year

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:47.740
as more conservative speakers were drawn to
the Berkeley campus.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:52.580
The students we talked to said that the violence
was carried out almost entirely by outsiders.

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:57.340
A lot of outside media comes in and sees,
you know, something very sensational

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:00.959
like stuff on fire in the middle of Sprout
Plaza where we're standing

00:02:00.959 --> 00:02:04.779
and takes this to represent that young people
are not interested in civil discourse

00:02:04.779 --> 00:02:06.349
which is not true.

00:02:06.349 --> 00:02:08.280
Specifically if you go back to the Milo riots,

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:11.000
one of the things that really annoys me about
all of this,

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:16.300
is that Berkeley students are blamed for all
of the damage that happened to our campus.

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:21.280
There's just a small group of, like, people,
Antifa you've probably heard of them

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:25.160
and they just come up here, mess everything
up, we get blamed for it

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:27.700
and then all the students are just like 'aww
that sucks.'

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:34.820
Antifa, short for anti-fascist, is a decentralized
movement that rejects the tactic of non-violence

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:40.580
and vows to fight whatever or whomever it
identifies as fascist by any means necessary.

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:42.760
They are taking advantage of the Berkeley
campus

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:44.840
and the Berkeley students and the Berkeley people

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:49.120
and we're getting a rap for something that's
not associated necessarily with us.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:52.060
Pretty much resorting to this force, this
violence,

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:54.800
pepper spraying just Trump supporters

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:59.180
who did nothing other than express their political opinions

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:02.400
that's not what we should be doing at all.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:07.920
And I want to say that that's not what Antifa
should be doing either.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:12.580
Most of the students we spoke to condemned
the group and it's tactics.

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:15.960
But they were also reluctant to condemn political
violence altogether.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:17.900
I personally wouldn't throw a stone through
a window

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:21.540
but I would never criticize the psyche of
someone who does.

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:28.840
I think it's hard for there to be very big
change without some sort of violent aspect to it

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:34.340
whether that's property destruction or human
life which is like the sad reality that we live in.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:38.880
That speech can be considered violence, inviting
people like Milo Yiannopoulos

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:43.120
creates an unsafe environment for the people
that he is speaking against.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:48.340
Property damage is absolutely justifiable.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:53.740
Dr Martin Luther King Jr. once said that riots
are the language of the voiceless.

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:58.900
To kind of hearken back to what MLK said,
a way for the unheard to make their voices amplified.

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:03.020
A riot is the language of the unheard.

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:06.400
That famous quote has been widely misinterpreted.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:08.220
King wasn't condoning riots,

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:11.180
he was saying that in response to racial oppression,

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:13.900
it was understandable, though counterproductive.

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:17.700
And that nonviolent protest is the only path
to social change.

00:04:17.709 --> 00:04:25.640
I think for the negro to turn to violence
would be both impractical and immoral.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:30.440
Putting aside the question of whether or not
preemptive political violence is moral,

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:32.240
is it actually effective?

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:37.400
In 'Why Civil Resistance Works' political
scientists Erica Chenoweth and Maria Stephan

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:39.980
compared violent and non violent movements
throughout history

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:44.840
and found that non violent ones were more
than twice as effective at achieving their goals.

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:46.620
Princeton researcher Omar Wasow

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:51.389
found that the non violent protests of the
early civil rights movement changed minds

00:04:51.389 --> 00:04:54.220
while the left wing riots in the late 1960s

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:57.920
likely tipped the presidential election in
favor of Richard Nixon.

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:01.220
And if Antifa members still want to punch
so called Nazis,

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:04.400
they should pay attention to the work of historian
Laurie Marhoefer

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:09.090
who studies real Nazis and says the party
often rallied close to it's adversaries

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:10.860
in order to provoke them.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:14.940
Drawing a violent reaction that swayed public
opinion in its favor.

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:17.900
Today's provocateurs are nothing like Adolf
Hitler,

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:20.080
but there's no doubt that Milo Yiannopoulos

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:22.800
chooses to come to places like Berkeley for
a reason.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:25.720
You hear it a lot, that you're intentionally
trying to provoke

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:27.600
the kind of response we saw last night.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:31.100
I mean, what my response is 'so what? who
cares?'

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:32.870
I'm an entertainer, I'm a performer.

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:37.260
I personally think that the Milo protest caught
on so much fire

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:40.480
because of the sheer intensity of the people
protesting against him.

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:44.440
It sounds like you're saying the best way
to deal with someone like Milo is to ignore him.

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:46.280
Well that's what you do with trolls, right?

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:47.220
Don't feed the trolls?

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:48.080
Don't feed the trolls!

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:49.040
We got played.

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:51.200
The response to the assault on Richard Spencer

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:54.920
was hardly the only instance of a blase media
response to violence.

00:05:54.920 --> 00:06:00.280
The brutal attack on Senator Rand Paul, which
left him with bruised lungs and six broken ribs

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:03.540
was met with jokes and suggestions that he
had it coming

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:05.520
because of his libertarian views.

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:10.440
Is it justifiable to act violently towards
someone who comes to campus

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:14.080
and is advocating against universal health
care?

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:17.080
I don't think I'd punch that person, yeah.

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:18.620
Is it okay for someone else to?

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:26.260
I wouldn't encourage it.

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:30.160
If it happened I don't know that I would feel
any particular remorse.

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:33.740
But you don't have to feel remorse for what
happens to your political opponents

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:35.640
to condemn violence against them.

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:39.440
Because punching them will most likely further
their cause.

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:43.260
The right of protest is a crucial one that
deserves protection.

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:48.720
But it's vital that anyone seeking political
change seriously consider these questions.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:51.500
Is political violence morally justifiable?

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:54.680
And if so, is it really worth it?

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:57.340
If we say that it's okay to punch a Nazi,

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:02.780
do we risk that umbrella of who's a Nazi becoming
a little too large?

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:06.180
No.

